    "result_text": "The build is green. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-registration limit following the existing quota-rule patterns in the codebase (mirroring the per-city capacity rule):\n\n1. **`DailyRegistrationLimitException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` thrown by the service layer, documented as mapping to HTTP 429.\n\n2. **`ClinicServiceImpl.java`** \u2014 added `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100` and an `assertDailyRegistrationLimit(owner)` check invoked from `saveOwner(...)` for new owners. It counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals the new owner's registration date (excluding the owner being saved by id) and throws when the count is \u2265 100. The check runs after the registration date is resolved (defaulting to `LocalDate.now()` when none is supplied).\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` that maps `DailyRegistrationLimitException` to `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429), returning a `ProblemDetail` consistent with the other handlers.\n\nThe service-layer placement means both the V1 and V2 controllers are covered. The acceptance test (`coreRejectsOverDailyLimit`: create 100, expect the 101st to be `429`) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0).",
