        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the existing patterns of this codebase:\n\n- **`DailyRegistrationLimitException`** mirrors the sibling `CityAtCapacityException` exactly \u2014 same package (`service`), same `RuntimeException` base, same constructor-builds-message shape.\n- **`assertDailyRegistrationLimit`** is structurally identical to the established `assertCityHasCapacity` / `assertHouseholdIsUnique` private helpers: same `findAll().stream()` \u2192 exclude-self-by-id \u2192 filter \u2192 `count() >= MAX` idiom, same early null-guard style, same Javadoc tone. The `findAll().stream()` approach is the pervasive idiom throughout this service (namesakes, city, household, customer-code), so matching it is the right call rather than introducing a one-off repository query.\n- **`MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY`** sits next to `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY` with a parallel doc comment.\n- **The handler** in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` follows the same `@ExceptionHandler` + `detailBuild(...)` + `ResponseEntity.status(...)` template as the other handlers, with a matching `ERROR_*` constant.\n- **Placement in `saveOwner`** is correct and necessary: the check is guarded by `owner.isNew()` like its peers, and sits *after* the `registrationDate` defaulting so the value it counts on is populated.\n\nLogic is in the right unit (service enforces the rule, advice maps to 429, exception carries the signal), nothing is duplicated, and no class is turning into a catch-all. A maintainer here would find this entirely natural."
